1
00:00:00,067 --> 00:00:02,426
On a 16 GB card,
you turn on a single setting,

2
00:00:02,446 --> 00:00:04,031
and your AI writes twice as fast:

3
00:00:04,283 --> 00:00:06,350
from 29 to 64 tokens per second.

4
00:00:06,473 --> 00:00:08,989
Same file, same card,
and nothing new to buy.

5
00:00:09,016 --> 00:00:10,847
And that's what 6 published
measurements find,

6
00:00:10,867 --> 00:00:12,754
across 5 models of 16 GB cards:

7
00:00:12,885 --> 00:00:15,264
between 1.9 and 2.3 times faster.

8
00:00:15,474 --> 00:00:16,995
So could your card be held back,

9
00:00:17,015 --> 00:00:18,622
without you even knowing it?

10
00:00:18,710 --> 00:00:20,268
And careful, this applies to writing too,

11
00:00:20,288 --> 00:00:23,859
on Qwen 3.8 27B, a model that
fits entirely on your card.

12
00:00:24,030 --> 00:00:27,066
So in this video, we'll look at
the setting that lets you double

13
00:00:27,086 --> 00:00:28,378
the speed of your graphics card

14
00:00:28,398 --> 00:00:30,328
and the engine that gets you there.

15
00:00:30,396 --> 00:00:33,578
And above all, everything to avoid
so you don't lose what you gained:

16
00:00:33,724 --> 00:00:36,954
a conversation that's too long, and
you've already lost a third of your speed.

17
00:00:37,046 --> 00:00:37,941
And stay until the end,

18
00:00:37,961 --> 00:00:39,561
because we're going to debunk a setting

19
00:00:39,581 --> 00:00:41,771
you may have turned on to go faster.

20
00:00:42,219 --> 00:00:43,699
The setting that doubles it is MTP,

21
00:00:43,826 --> 00:00:45,656
a small extra module inside the model.

22
00:00:45,808 --> 00:00:49,192
Basically, it's like the suggestions
on your phone's keyboard:

23
00:00:49,374 --> 00:00:53,401
the model suggests several tokens ahead,
then checks them all at once.

24
00:00:53,683 --> 00:00:56,948
And when it guessed right,
it writes several for the price of one.

25
00:00:57,121 --> 00:00:59,358
However, this only affects writing.

26
00:00:59,489 --> 00:01:03,332
Reading your prompt is slowed down
by MTP in the first tests from May.

27
00:01:03,466 --> 00:01:07,350
On a 5080, it goes from
53 to 101 tokens per second.

28
00:01:07,413 --> 00:01:10,469
On a 4060 Ti, from 17.6 to 40,

29
00:01:10,583 --> 00:01:13,533
and even at AMD, on an RX 9060 XT,

30
00:01:13,707 --> 00:01:15,455
from 15 to almost 29.

31
00:01:15,615 --> 00:01:18,049
So even the Radeon
almost doubles its speed.

32
00:01:18,461 --> 00:01:21,606
Except 5 of these 6 measurements
come from the same online table,

33
00:01:21,626 --> 00:01:23,116
with the same test script.

34
00:01:23,394 --> 00:01:26,781
So the most solid one is
the first one, the 29 to 64:

35
00:01:26,874 --> 00:01:28,846
it was measured separately, 5 times.

36
00:01:29,027 --> 00:01:30,031
And it finds the same thing.

37
00:01:30,219 --> 00:01:32,689
In fact, in the table,
that doubling is an average over code,

38
00:01:32,786 --> 00:01:35,410
text and commands,
with reasoning turned off.

39
00:01:35,590 --> 00:01:39,083
On text alone, it drops
to 1.8 times on a 4060 Ti.

40
00:01:39,425 --> 00:01:41,201
Code is where it gains the most.

41
00:01:41,380 --> 00:01:44,934
And on a mixture-of-experts model,
measurements drop to 1.5 times at best.

42
00:01:45,204 --> 00:01:47,291
So why do I say your card is held back?

43
00:01:47,414 --> 00:01:50,401
Because in llama.cpp,
MTP doesn't turn on by itself.

44
00:01:50,498 --> 00:01:53,516
You have to ask for it, and you need
a file that contains the module,

45
00:01:53,538 --> 00:01:55,097
a small extra piece of model.

46
00:01:55,302 --> 00:01:58,768
LM Studio has handled
it since version 0.4.22.

47
00:01:58,890 --> 00:02:02,171
You'll find it in the settings
panel of the model you selected.

48
00:02:02,348 --> 00:02:05,888
So whichever one you're on, go check
that it's really turned on for you.

49
00:02:06,268 --> 00:02:09,190
But MTP has a price,
and it takes up space.

50
00:02:09,695 --> 00:02:13,294
According to the measurements,
1 to 2.5 GB of extra VRAM.

51
00:02:13,468 --> 00:02:16,224
And on a 16 GB card, that's
exactly where it gets tight.

52
00:02:16,617 --> 00:02:20,130
So the real setting number one is that
the model fits entirely in your card.

53
00:02:20,369 --> 00:02:24,482
On a 16 GB 4080, with an
older 27-billion Qwen:

54
00:02:24,502 --> 00:02:27,698
a 15 GB file that spills over
a bit, 20 tokens per second.

55
00:02:27,890 --> 00:02:32,278
An 11.5 GB file that fits completely,
45 tokens per second.

56
00:02:32,457 --> 00:02:34,826
More than double, just by
picking a single smaller file.

57
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:37,554
And if part of the model leaves
the card, it's a freefall.

58
00:02:37,814 --> 00:02:42,170
On a 5060 Ti, someone deliberately
left 8 layers out of 48

59
00:02:42,190 --> 00:02:46,393
of a smaller model: it drops from
43 to 12.5 tokens per second.

60
00:02:46,488 --> 00:02:49,309
In fact, the part left
in RAM runs much slower,

61
00:02:49,356 --> 00:02:50,530
so the card waits for it.

62
00:02:50,728 --> 00:02:53,045
On the other hand,
a smaller file is more compressed,

63
00:02:53,118 --> 00:02:56,265
and none of these measurements
says what you lose in quality.

64
00:02:56,576 --> 00:02:58,906
But in reality, these gains don't add up.

65
00:02:59,174 --> 00:03:01,085
If MTP makes your file spill over,

66
00:03:01,242 --> 00:03:02,922
the drop eats what it gains you.

67
00:03:03,053 --> 00:03:06,148
So first, a file that
fits with room for MTP,

68
00:03:06,222 --> 00:03:07,521
then you turn on MTP.

69
00:03:08,025 --> 00:03:10,558
Well, with just these 2 settings,
your model can write

70
00:03:10,578 --> 00:03:12,712
twice as fast on the
card you already have.

71
00:03:12,898 --> 00:03:14,523
So before buying a bigger card,

72
00:03:14,589 --> 00:03:18,423
especially right now with the crazy
prices, they've completely skyrocketed,

73
00:03:18,453 --> 00:03:22,088
try optimizing the one you already
have and turning on a few settings.

74
00:03:22,108 --> 00:03:24,392
If this video saved you from
a bad purchase, subscribe.

75
00:03:24,844 --> 00:03:26,508
And the engine that gets you there,

76
00:03:26,605 --> 00:03:28,558
the program that runs the model.

77
00:03:28,709 --> 00:03:30,423
The most popular is llama.cpp.

78
00:03:30,588 --> 00:03:33,325
All 6 of the first
measurements were made on it.

79
00:03:33,373 --> 00:03:35,076
When everything fits in
the card, it's great:

80
00:03:35,096 --> 00:03:39,379
Ollama was measured 10% slower
than llama.cpp, on a 5060 Ti,

81
00:03:39,411 --> 00:03:41,756
and up to 30% slower on a 3090.

82
00:03:42,133 --> 00:03:45,067
And LM Studio was nearly
tied with llama.cpp:

83
00:03:45,304 --> 00:03:47,472
76.8 versus 77.

84
00:03:47,791 --> 00:03:51,050
So if you're on LM Studio or llama.cpp,
like in the previous video,

85
00:03:51,225 --> 00:03:52,415
on that front, you're fine.

86
00:03:52,810 --> 00:03:56,023
And even with an optimized engine,
an update can slow you down.

87
00:03:56,217 --> 00:03:58,442
On a 5080 with the Strata engine,

88
00:03:58,609 --> 00:04:02,195
a user lost 25% moving to a newer version,

89
00:04:02,313 --> 00:04:05,417
and its version
0.2.14, released on November 15,

90
00:04:05,616 --> 00:04:08,705
reads your prompt faster,
but writes about 10% slower

91
00:04:08,734 --> 00:04:11,351
on a 5070 Ti, according
to the project itself.

92
00:04:11,546 --> 00:04:13,212
That's why you should note your version,

93
00:04:13,232 --> 00:04:15,345
to know where a slowdown comes from.

94
00:04:15,477 --> 00:04:17,727
The ones from my previous
video are on screen.

95
00:04:18,023 --> 00:04:19,733
And what if your model
is too big for your card?

96
00:04:19,824 --> 00:04:21,359
In the previous video, I showed you:

97
00:04:21,379 --> 00:04:23,265
the RAM full, and the
disk starting to read.

98
00:04:23,463 --> 00:04:26,155
On a mixture-of-experts model,
in llama.cpp, there's a setting for you:

99
00:04:26,292 --> 00:04:28,727
where the experts go,
those little specialists in the model.

100
00:04:28,918 --> 00:04:31,560
Which ones stay on the card,
and which go to RAM.

101
00:04:31,637 --> 00:04:34,227
The right setting is to keep as
many on the card as possible,

102
00:04:34,322 --> 00:04:35,537
right before it crashes.

103
00:04:35,697 --> 00:04:38,775
On a 12 GB 3060, it gained 12%.

104
00:04:38,866 --> 00:04:41,721
On a 16 GB 5060 Ti, with the expert cache

105
00:04:41,741 --> 00:04:44,849
that arrived in llama.cpp
in October, 29% more.

106
00:04:45,090 --> 00:04:47,574
Just one measurement each,
so take them with a grain of salt,

107
00:04:47,594 --> 00:04:50,247
and their PCs had 32 and 64 GB of RAM.

108
00:04:50,461 --> 00:04:51,426
Now, what's the catch?

109
00:04:51,573 --> 00:04:54,022
The longer your conversation,
the slower it gets.

110
00:04:54,096 --> 00:04:57,208
Remember the first measurement,
at 29 tokens per second?

111
00:04:57,314 --> 00:05:00,627
From 64,000 tokens of
conversation, it drops to 20.

112
00:05:00,810 --> 00:05:02,172
So a third of the speed is gone,

113
00:05:02,192 --> 00:05:04,753
and with MTP, the 64 becomes 39.

114
00:05:04,956 --> 00:05:07,783
MTP still doubles it,
but everything goes down.

115
00:05:07,956 --> 00:05:09,102
And it can get much worse.

116
00:05:09,165 --> 00:05:12,181
In fact, the conversation's
memory is stored on your card too.

117
00:05:12,201 --> 00:05:15,746
And the 4080 that reached
45 with a small file?

118
00:05:15,766 --> 00:05:19,880
At 128,000 tokens of conversation,
this memory pushes the model out,

119
00:05:19,900 --> 00:05:22,243
and the card drops below
10 tokens per second.

120
00:05:22,305 --> 00:05:25,679
In short, 2 dead-simple moves: a new
conversation as soon as you change topic,

121
00:05:25,699 --> 00:05:28,602
and a conversation size set as
tight as possible in your engine.

122
00:05:28,694 --> 00:05:31,044
And if you really need
a long conversation,

123
00:05:31,145 --> 00:05:33,088
there's one last setting to try.

124
00:05:33,368 --> 00:05:35,192
That setting is compressing the KV cache,

125
00:05:35,212 --> 00:05:36,765
which is the conversation's memory.

126
00:05:36,902 --> 00:05:39,465
Do you think that can
make it write faster?

127
00:05:39,618 --> 00:05:41,884
Keep your answer,
and tell me in the comments.

128
00:05:41,945 --> 00:05:45,274
On a 5060 Ti, with a
small 7-billion model,

129
00:05:45,294 --> 00:05:48,213
writing is 6.6% slower at 8 bits,

130
00:05:48,233 --> 00:05:50,292
and 1.8% slower at 4 bits.

131
00:05:50,370 --> 00:05:54,535
And on an NVIDIA machine built for AI,
at 4 bits, at 64,000 tokens,

132
00:05:54,555 --> 00:05:55,689
it loses 35%.

133
00:05:55,709 --> 00:05:57,524
So for speed, it's useless.

134
00:05:57,698 --> 00:05:58,953
But it makes room.

135
00:05:59,143 --> 00:06:03,066
On a 16 GB laptop 3080, with Qwen 3.8 27B,

136
00:06:03,092 --> 00:06:06,293
you go from 32,000 to over
128,000 tokens of conversation.

137
00:06:06,406 --> 00:06:09,521
So it fits a lot more
conversation in your card

138
00:06:09,554 --> 00:06:11,291
before the model gets pushed out.

139
00:06:11,361 --> 00:06:13,511
It doesn't save you from
the lost third of speed,

140
00:06:13,541 --> 00:06:15,689
since the first measurement already had

141
00:06:15,871 --> 00:06:19,232
the compressed cache. But it can
avoid the violent drop in speed.

142
00:06:19,543 --> 00:06:22,101
But you also have to
be careful with 4 bits.

143
00:06:22,219 --> 00:06:24,728
On the 7-billion model from earlier,

144
00:06:24,762 --> 00:06:28,160
the 8-bit answer is 82%
similar to the original,

145
00:06:28,244 --> 00:06:29,976
at 4 bits, only 8%.

146
00:06:30,047 --> 00:06:33,957
And at least 3 of the first 6 measurements
were at 4 bits on a much bigger model:

147
00:06:33,998 --> 00:06:36,362
what that cost in quality, nobody said.

148
00:06:36,629 --> 00:06:39,060
So if everything fits at 8 bits
on your end, stay at 8 bits.

149
00:06:39,242 --> 00:06:40,749
There, that's the little
setting that lets you

150
00:06:40,769 --> 00:06:42,740
last longer in your conversation.

151
00:06:43,065 --> 00:06:44,741
So now, what do you need to be able to

152
00:06:44,929 --> 00:06:46,894
properly tune your engine and your model,

153
00:06:47,083 --> 00:06:48,968
in order: a file that fits in your card,

154
00:06:48,988 --> 00:06:50,718
with room for MTP,

155
00:06:50,798 --> 00:06:53,523
then MTP turned on, especially for code,

156
00:06:53,586 --> 00:06:56,071
llama.cpp or LM Studio,
rather than Ollama,

157
00:06:56,095 --> 00:06:57,421
and if your model spills over,

158
00:06:57,577 --> 00:06:59,045
set where the experts go,

159
00:06:59,092 --> 00:07:01,360
a fresh conversation
when this one gets long,

160
00:07:01,380 --> 00:07:03,657
and also the 8-bit cache
if you're short on room.

161
00:07:03,910 --> 00:07:05,300
And don't forget to note your version

162
00:07:05,320 --> 00:07:07,335
to compare whether things change or not,

163
00:07:07,355 --> 00:07:10,116
or whether you may have
hit a bug with yours.

164
00:07:10,367 --> 00:07:12,130
So, is your card held back or not?

165
00:07:12,318 --> 00:07:14,945
If MTP is off by default,
or if your file spills over,

166
00:07:14,965 --> 00:07:16,385
your card is completely held back.

167
00:07:16,439 --> 00:07:19,363
It's fixed in a few clicks,
tonight, with this video,

168
00:07:19,395 --> 00:07:20,529
without buying anything new.

169
00:07:20,814 --> 00:07:23,496
And if your card only has 8 GB,
we'll try to explore later

170
00:07:23,516 --> 00:07:26,863
how to fit an AI model on 8 GB of VRAM.

171
00:07:26,980 --> 00:07:28,856
Alright, I hope you liked this video,

172
00:07:28,876 --> 00:07:30,298
don't forget to subscribe to the channel,

173
00:07:30,318 --> 00:07:32,649
and if you can, boost the video.

174
00:07:32,669 --> 00:07:34,314
See you next time. Bye!

